Based on my analysis, here's my assessment. A note on method and confidence: this radiograph is heavily compressed/blurred, and the trabecular bone is diffusely mottled throughout the jaws, which makes discrete periapical radiolucencies hard to separate from normal anatomy (nasal fossa, mental foramina, mandibular canal, sinus floor). After multi-region contrast/zoom inspection, the only apical regions showing distinctive radiolucent bone change were the mandibular first-molar periapical areas bilaterally; I found no convincing well-defined (PAI 4–5) lesion, and graded what I could see as PAI 3.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.646, 0.611, 0.716, 0.704], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.303, 0.611, 0.372, 0.704], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Caveat worth flagging plainly: given the image quality I could not reliably identify any PAI 4 or 5 lesion, so treat these as low-confidence PAI 3 calls, not a confident complete enumeration.
